W: 1. How pleasant is the wind tonight,
W: I feel some drops of rain.
W: I never had but one true love,
W: And in greenwood he was slain.
W: I'll do so much for my true love,
W: As any young maid may.
W: I'll sit and mourn all on his grave,
W: For a twelve-month and one day.
W:
W: 2. The twelve-month and one day being up
W: The ghost began to speak.
W: Why sit you here and mourn for me
W: And will not let me sleep?
W: What do you want of me, sweetheart
W: Or what is it you crave?
W: I want one kiss of your lily-white lips,
W: And that is all I crave.
W:
W: 3. My lips they are as cold as clay,
W: My breath be heavy and strong.
W: You have one kiss of my lily-white lips,
W: Your life will not be long.
W: My life be't long or short, sweetheart,
W: But that is all I crave,
W: Then I shall be along with you,
W: A-lying in my grave.
W:
W: 4. 'Twas down in Cupid's Garden,
W: Where you and I would walk.
W: The finest ﬂower that ever was there
W: Is withered to a stalk.
W: Is withered to a stalk, sweetheart,
W: The flower will never return,
W: And since I lost my own sweetheart,
W: What can I do but mourn?
W:
W: 5. Oh don't you see the fire sweetheart
W: The fire that burns so blue?
W: Where my poor soul tormented is,
W: While I stay here with you.
W: And if you wasn't my own sweetheart
W: As I know well you be,
W: I'd rend you up in pieces small,
W: As leaves upon the tree.
W:
W: (Repeat last two lines of music with these lyrics:)
W:
W: Mourn not for me, my dearest dear
W: Mourn not for me, I crave.
W: I must leave you and all the world,
W: And turn into my grave.
